/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Port 1 is the input, port 2 the through, port 3 the coupled and port 4 the isolated port, all matched.
   - P₁ is the power fed into port 1 and P₃, P₄ the powers leaving ports 3 and 4.
   - A 10 dB coupler sends one tenth of the input to port 3; a 20 dB coupler one hundredth.
   - An ideal coupler is matched, reciprocal and lossless, with the isolated port receiving nothing.
   - rows and columns are ports 1 to 4; the coupled wave leads the through wave by 90°.
   - Two guides share a broad wall with two small holes λg/4 apart.
   - The cancellation is exact only where the spacing is λg/4, so directivity falls away from the design frequency; multi-hole couplers with graded hole sizes keep it over a wider band.
   - A hybrid ring is a closed ring of line with a circumference of 1.5λg and four ports.
   - A wave fed into a port runs both ways round the ring.
   - So port 1 is the sum port: fed there, ports 2 and 4 get half the power each, in phase, and port 3 gets nothing.
*/
