/* book Worked example 2: Q: A reflectometer uses a 20 dB coupler with 30 dB directivity to measure a load whose true return loss is 20 dB (|Γ| = 0.1). (a) Find |S₃₁|, |S₄₁| and the through loss of the coupler. (b) With 30 dBm incident, compare the reflected sample with the directivity leak at the same detector, and find the range of |Γ| the instrument may show.
   1. |S₃₁| = 10−20/20 = 0.1. Isolation I = 20 + 30 = 50 dB, so |S₄₁| = 10−50/20 = 0.00316.
   2. Lossless coupler: |S₂₁| = √(1 − 0.1² − 0.00316²) = 0.995, a through loss of 0.044 dB.
   3. Reflected wave: 30 − 20 = 10 dBm, coupled to the reverse detector at 10 − 20 = −10 dBm. The forward wave leaks there by the isolation: 30 − 50 = −20 dBm, only 10 dB below the wanted sample.
   4. In waves the detector sees 0.1|Γ| plus a leak of 0.1 × 0.0316 (30 dB is a ratio of 0.0316). They may add or subtract, so the reading of |Γ| lies between 0.1 − 0.0316 = 0.068 and 0.1 + 0.0316 = 0.132.
   5. In return loss: between −20 log₁₀ 0.132 = 17.6 dB and −20 log₁₀ 0.068 = 23.3 dB, for a load that is really 20 dB.
   Answer: |S₃₁| = 0.1, |S₄₁| = 0.0032, through loss 0.044 dB. The 30 dB directivity lets the reading of a 20 dB return-loss load fall anywhere from 17.6 dB to 23.3 dB; measuring well-matched loads needs a directivity well above their return loss. */
