/* book exam traps: Mixing up coupling and directivity. Coupling compares port 3 with port 1; directivity compares port 3 with port 4; isolation compares port 1 with port 4, and I = C + D. | Using 10 log with |S| values. |S| is a wave ratio, so C = −20 log₁₀|S₃₁|; with powers use 10 log₁₀(P₁/P₃). Mixing them doubles or halves every answer. | Numbering a rat-race wrongly. The isolated pair is the two ports whose paths differ by λg/2; with the 3λg/4 arc between ports 2 and 3, ports 1 and 3 are isolated and port 1 is the sum port. | Saying the 3λg/4 arc "gives 180°". It is 270° of line; the 180° is its difference from a λg/4 arc. | Assuming port 4 receives zero. Finite directivity leaks power there, which limits how small a reflection a reflectometer can measure. */
